00:01:15.094 --> 00:01:18.050
- You don't know everything, all right?

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:19.926
Let's just get it right
out there on the table.

00:01:19.926 --> 00:01:22.237
I meet so many young entrepreneurs,

00:01:22.237 --> 00:01:25.054
they think they know everything,

00:01:25.054 --> 00:01:27.348
they want to know everything,

00:01:27.348 --> 00:01:29.619
they want to be the best at everything

00:01:29.619 --> 00:01:34.067
inside their company and
guys that's just not reality.

00:01:34.067 --> 00:01:37.656
Okay, you have to understand,
winning in business

00:01:37.656 --> 00:01:42.435
is not about you personally
as the CEO of a company

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:45.185
being the best at every skillset.

00:01:46.567 --> 00:01:48.926
Dude, I can't put together graphics,

00:01:48.926 --> 00:01:51.902
I can't put together videos, I can't,

00:01:51.902 --> 00:01:53.507
there are certain things in my company

00:01:53.507 --> 00:01:57.628
that I have no clue on
how to do but that's okay.

00:01:57.628 --> 00:01:59.204
You know what my job is?

00:01:59.204 --> 00:02:02.508
My job is to go out and
find people that can do that

00:02:02.508 --> 00:02:06.388
far better than myself so
that we can win as a team.

00:02:06.388 --> 00:02:09.779
And this attitude of
I've got to be the best,

00:02:09.779 --> 00:02:12.803
and I've got to win and
I've got to, you know,

00:02:12.803 --> 00:02:15.346
and I'm the best at everything
and I know more than you

00:02:15.346 --> 00:02:18.415
and blah, blah, blah, blah,
that makes horrible leadership.

00:02:18.415 --> 00:02:20.051
Nobody respects that.

00:02:20.051 --> 00:02:22.179
Let me tell you what people do respect.

00:02:22.179 --> 00:02:24.919
People respect when
you look 'em in the eye

00:02:24.919 --> 00:02:28.156
and you say, "Hey, what do you think?"

00:02:28.156 --> 00:02:30.641
And then, they give you an
answer and then it's your job

00:02:30.641 --> 00:02:35.170
to decide whether or not
on an honest playing field,

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:37.360
whether or not that is
the best thing to do

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:38.878
or whether or not somebody else

00:02:38.878 --> 00:02:41.195
has the best thing to do, all right?

00:02:41.195 --> 00:02:42.541
And that might be your idea,

00:02:42.541 --> 00:02:44.572
it might be another employee's idea,

00:02:44.572 --> 00:02:48.770
it doesn't matter as long
as the best idea comes about

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:52.203
and you are able to
decide which one that is.

00:02:52.203 --> 00:02:53.679
You guys have to understand

00:02:53.679 --> 00:02:55.476
and get over your selves.

00:02:55.476 --> 00:02:57.334
Being in business, owning a business,

00:02:57.334 --> 00:02:59.991
is not about being right, okay?

00:02:59.991 --> 00:03:01.588
I see this also in the same thing

00:03:01.588 --> 00:03:05.379
as when people develop
products for themselves

00:03:05.379 --> 00:03:08.872
versus understanding what
the market likes, all right?

00:03:08.872 --> 00:03:10.879
The same people who I just talked about

00:03:10.879 --> 00:03:11.990
that can't accept the fact

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:13.833
that they're not always the best

00:03:13.833 --> 00:03:15.272
and that they're not always right

00:03:15.272 --> 00:03:18.211
or the same people that
develop products for the market

00:03:18.211 --> 00:03:19.970
based upon what they like

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:23.017
and not based upon what the market likes.

00:03:23.017 --> 00:03:25.956
And you gusy, all of you
who fall in those groups,

00:03:25.956 --> 00:03:28.273
you better learn to do
what I'm telling you to do

00:03:28.273 --> 00:03:30.074
or you're going to be out of business.

00:03:30.074 --> 00:03:34.158
Because dude big egos, over-inflated egos,

00:03:34.158 --> 00:03:36.166
they come from insecurity, all right?

00:03:36.166 --> 00:03:38.419
The only reason that
you would act that way

00:03:38.419 --> 00:03:40.741
is that if you're actually
insecure about the amount

00:03:40.741 --> 00:03:44.515
that you truly know so you
over inflate, puff your chest,

00:03:44.515 --> 00:03:47.545
feel like you need to
be right and guess what,

00:03:47.545 --> 00:03:50.426
you're usually wrong, your
employees don't respect you,

00:03:50.426 --> 00:03:52.056
your peers don't respect you,

00:03:52.056 --> 00:03:53.360
and this goes for you people

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:55.272
who are in an office environment

00:03:55.272 --> 00:03:59.069
working for another
company, owning a company,

00:03:59.069 --> 00:04:00.418
it goes for everybody listening.

00:04:00.418 --> 00:04:03.912
You're not always going to be right.

00:04:03.912 --> 00:04:04.912
That's okay.

00:04:05.746 --> 00:04:08.443
The best leaders in the
world are not the ones

00:04:08.443 --> 00:04:11.072
that are the smartest or the
ones that are most right.

00:04:11.072 --> 00:04:13.731
They're the ones that can
decipher who's the best

00:04:13.731 --> 00:04:17.442
at which job, put in
the right places to win

00:04:17.442 --> 00:04:21.320
and then also decide from a humble place

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:23.374
and humble meaning understanding

00:04:23.374 --> 00:04:25.957
that you don't know everything,

00:04:26.867 --> 00:04:31.196
which idea is going to be
the best for everybody, okay?

00:04:31.196 --> 00:04:34.176
That's your job, you're
the captain of the team,

00:04:34.176 --> 00:04:37.671
you're the CEO, you're
the captain of the ship.

00:04:37.671 --> 00:04:39.802
If you're going to steer
the ship into rocks,

00:04:39.802 --> 00:04:43.895
just because you argued
with your first mate

00:04:43.895 --> 00:04:48.724
about your path being
right, you're a idiot, okay?

00:04:48.724 --> 00:04:50.652
It's okay to not have all the answers,

00:04:50.652 --> 00:04:52.507
it's okay to not be right,

00:04:52.507 --> 00:04:54.394
it's okay to admit that you weren't right

00:04:54.394 --> 00:04:57.052
and in fact, if you want
the respect of your team

00:04:57.052 --> 00:05:00.362
and your peers, you will be
willing and able to admit

00:05:00.362 --> 00:05:04.352
that you made mistakes, the
minute that you make 'em.

00:05:04.352 --> 00:05:06.399
Here's the deal, you want
to know why most people

00:05:06.399 --> 00:05:07.822
can never get anywhere in life,

00:05:07.822 --> 00:05:11.388
why most people never
live, never experience,

00:05:11.388 --> 00:05:15.279
never build, never do anything worthwhile

00:05:15.279 --> 00:05:18.076
in life besides being average

00:05:18.076 --> 00:05:21.537
and let me know, an average broke.

00:05:21.537 --> 00:05:22.862
You know why?

00:05:22.862 --> 00:05:27.029
Because most people's egos
are so inflated and so fragile

00:05:28.969 --> 00:05:31.554
that they won't allow people to see them

00:05:31.554 --> 00:05:33.519
be bad at something.

00:05:33.519 --> 00:05:36.171
Okay, let that sink in.

00:05:36.171 --> 00:05:39.469
You never done something
before, you expect yourself

00:05:39.469 --> 00:05:42.259
to be awesome at it, you
haven't done the work,

00:05:42.259 --> 00:05:44.010
you haven't put it the time,

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:46.446
you haven't put in the study hours,

00:05:46.446 --> 00:05:48.738
you haven't had the experience

00:05:48.738 --> 00:05:51.143
but you expect to be good.

00:05:51.143 --> 00:05:53.021
And because you expect
to be good but you know

00:05:53.021 --> 00:05:56.366
you're not going to be
good, you don't do anything.

00:05:56.366 --> 00:05:58.654
Explain that to me, okay?

00:05:58.654 --> 00:06:01.756
You want to be somebody who succeeds,

00:06:01.756 --> 00:06:03.759
you want to be somebody
who builds something,

00:06:03.759 --> 00:06:07.604
you want to be somebody who
people write books about one day

00:06:07.604 --> 00:06:10.981
you're going to have to
be okay with being bad at

00:06:10.981 --> 00:06:12.898
for a long time.

00:06:12.898 --> 00:06:14.079
When I first started in business,

00:06:14.079 --> 00:06:16.783
I didn't even know what
the I was selling, okay?

00:06:16.783 --> 00:06:18.644
I remember the first person coming in,

00:06:18.644 --> 00:06:21.655
they came in for a product
that I didn't even,

00:06:21.655 --> 00:06:24.469
it was called Cell-Tech by
a company called MuscleTech,

00:06:24.469 --> 00:06:26.586
they came in, the dude
obviously didn't buy it,

00:06:26.586 --> 00:06:29.203
it wasn't our first sale, he
came and asked you about it.

00:06:29.203 --> 00:06:31.612
I don't even know what
the fuck it was, okay?

00:06:31.612 --> 00:06:33.070
But I didn't care

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:35.345
'cause I was going to
figure it out, all right?

00:06:35.345 --> 00:06:37.896
And that's the mentality
you guys have to have.

00:06:37.896 --> 00:06:41.216
You have to be okay with
looking like an idiot

00:06:41.216 --> 00:06:42.965
for a long time.

00:06:42.965 --> 00:06:46.428
And a lot of you guys, you'd
rather be too cool for school,

00:06:46.428 --> 00:06:47.652
you'd rather be the cool kids

00:06:47.652 --> 00:06:50.146
at the high school lunch table,

00:06:50.146 --> 00:06:53.626
than be the person who actually goes out,

00:06:53.626 --> 00:06:55.581
takes a leap of faith,

00:06:55.581 --> 00:06:58.104
decides they're going
to try something new,

00:06:58.104 --> 00:07:00.761
and actually becomes an
expert at it over time.

00:07:00.761 --> 00:07:04.928
Guys, you have to be okay
not being good, all right?

00:07:07.454 --> 00:07:10.805
Every valuable lesson that
you're going to come across

00:07:10.805 --> 00:07:15.528
in your life is going to
come from you something up

00:07:15.528 --> 00:07:18.117
A.K.A. failing, all right?

00:07:18.117 --> 00:07:21.302
And it, I hate failing
as much as the next guy.

00:07:21.302 --> 00:07:23.633
Dude, I probably hold the
record for the most broken

00:07:23.633 --> 00:07:26.577
Nintendo controllers on
the face of the Earth

00:07:26.577 --> 00:07:29.458
because I hate losing and
if I ever find the person

00:07:29.458 --> 00:07:32.238
in Zelda, they're going
to get a knuckle sandwich

00:07:32.238 --> 00:07:34.363
'cause I could never beat that game

00:07:34.363 --> 00:07:35.737
but that's the point, okay?

00:07:35.737 --> 00:07:39.128
I hate to lose but the thing is

00:07:39.128 --> 00:07:43.369
is that once you understand
that every time you lose

00:07:43.369 --> 00:07:46.177
is an opportunity for you to
not only learn that lesson

00:07:46.177 --> 00:07:49.415
but take it a plus one step, okay?

00:07:49.415 --> 00:07:53.618
Listen to what I just said, a
plus one step past the lesson,

00:07:53.618 --> 00:07:56.443
you'll start to appreciate those lessons,

00:07:56.443 --> 00:08:00.308
you'll start to be okay
with not being good at.

00:08:00.308 --> 00:08:03.339
Guys, you don't want to be the guy

00:08:03.339 --> 00:08:07.384
that we talked about on the
podcast whose 37 years old,

00:08:07.384 --> 00:08:09.434
living in your mom's basement,

00:08:09.434 --> 00:08:12.296
yelling at her for a hot pocket, okay?

00:08:12.296 --> 00:08:14.454
Trolling people on the internet,

00:08:14.454 --> 00:08:16.061
you don't want to be that guy.

00:08:16.061 --> 00:08:17.805
And if you don't want to be that guy,

00:08:17.805 --> 00:08:19.808
you're going to have to understand

00:08:19.808 --> 00:08:23.063
that losing is a part
of ultimately winning.

00:08:23.063 --> 00:08:25.841
You're going to lose a
lot more than you can win

00:08:25.841 --> 00:08:28.419
and when you win, you're
going to figure out

00:08:28.419 --> 00:08:29.939
how to get better on top of it.

00:08:29.939 --> 00:08:33.669
If you're a true winner, they
don't celebrate their wins.

00:08:33.669 --> 00:08:35.438
True winners take the win

00:08:35.438 --> 00:08:37.459
and they say, "How could
I have even gotten better?

00:08:37.459 --> 00:08:39.116
"How could I ran the score up?

00:08:39.116 --> 00:08:41.732
"How could I have won by
twice as many points?"

00:08:41.732 --> 00:08:43.911
So you never really feel like you win

00:08:43.911 --> 00:08:46.236
and you have to be okay with that, okay?

00:08:46.236 --> 00:08:47.560
There's not one thing

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:49.006
that I don't look at in my life

00:08:49.006 --> 00:08:50.682
and say man how can I get better at that?

00:08:50.682 --> 00:08:51.590
How could I win at that?

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:54.387
How could I run the score up on that?

00:08:54.387 --> 00:08:55.698
That's how much I love to win

00:08:55.698 --> 00:08:59.049
but I look at every single loss,

00:08:59.049 --> 00:09:01.216
I've learned to look at every single loss

00:09:01.216 --> 00:09:03.223
as an opportunity of how to win more

00:09:03.223 --> 00:09:04.681
and that's what you need too.

00:09:04.681 --> 00:09:05.639
You don't look at it like,

00:09:05.639 --> 00:09:07.078
hey everybody's going to point and laugh

00:09:07.078 --> 00:09:08.888
and they might point and laugh.

00:09:08.888 --> 00:09:11.956
I could tell you a list
a mile long of people

00:09:11.956 --> 00:09:14.891
who have told me, "Andy,
you still got that

00:09:14.891 --> 00:09:15.932
"little vitamin shop?

00:09:15.932 --> 00:09:18.025
"Andy, what are you
trying to be Tony Robbins?

00:09:18.025 --> 00:09:21.173
"Andy, when you're
going to get a real job?

00:09:21.173 --> 00:09:26.033
Those are the same you asking
me for jobs now, all right?

00:09:26.033 --> 00:09:27.460
You guys have to understand

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:29.754
that if you are willing to be bad at,

00:09:29.754 --> 00:09:32.790
you will have the last
laugh on these same people

00:09:32.790 --> 00:09:34.794
that that we're talking about.

00:09:34.794 --> 00:09:36.431
I think it's important
for people to understand

00:09:36.431 --> 00:09:40.598
at any time you step out of
what's normal in society,

00:09:42.711 --> 00:09:45.729
you're going to have criticism.

00:09:45.729 --> 00:09:49.229
So many people follow the traditional plan

00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:52.905
with no thought whatsoever

00:09:52.905 --> 00:09:55.614
as to what they really want to do in life.

00:09:55.614 --> 00:09:58.820
They go to school, they go to high school,

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:01.375
they go to college, they get a degree,

00:10:01.375 --> 00:10:03.413
they get married, they have kids,

00:10:03.413 --> 00:10:05.628
then they wake up at 40 years old

00:10:05.628 --> 00:10:10.014
wondering, "What the happened
and where did my life go?"

00:10:10.014 --> 00:10:11.329
and when I think about those things,

00:10:11.329 --> 00:10:14.579
I think about how much criticism we got

00:10:16.149 --> 00:10:17.824
when we started our first company,

00:10:17.824 --> 00:10:20.157
when we're only 19 years old

00:10:21.126 --> 00:10:25.293
to do all the things that you're
supposed to do in society.

00:10:27.221 --> 00:10:29.353
All the people that used to tell us,

00:10:29.353 --> 00:10:30.660
"You guys waste your time,

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:32.418
"you need to get a degree,

00:10:32.418 --> 00:10:35.563
"you need to get a real job,

00:10:35.563 --> 00:10:40.119
"you need to quit wasting
your time with this hobby,"

00:10:40.119 --> 00:10:42.236
and those are the same people now that,

00:10:42.236 --> 00:10:44.489
they're telling us how
good of a job we did

00:10:44.489 --> 00:10:46.239
and how proud we are.

00:10:47.406 --> 00:10:49.406
I think about that a lot

00:10:50.452 --> 00:10:52.762
and I think people don't realize

00:10:52.762 --> 00:10:55.819
that the same criticism that they get

00:10:55.819 --> 00:10:59.751
for doing anything outside
of what's considered normal

00:10:59.751 --> 00:11:03.079
that goes for anybody that tries anything

00:11:03.079 --> 00:11:05.334
outside of what's normal.

00:11:05.334 --> 00:11:08.134
And I think people need to realize

00:11:08.134 --> 00:11:10.333
that people are going to talk,

00:11:10.333 --> 00:11:14.500
they're going to criticize
but what's more important,

00:11:15.429 --> 00:11:17.976
what's more important is that you do

00:11:17.976 --> 00:11:20.384
what it is you think you need to do

00:11:20.384 --> 00:11:22.801
instead of changing your path

00:11:26.037 --> 00:11:27.797
for everybody else's happiness

00:11:27.797 --> 00:11:30.786
because the reality is
the people who judge you

00:11:30.786 --> 00:11:32.381
on doing anything different

00:11:32.381 --> 00:11:35.686
are the same people who
can't do it themselves

00:11:35.686 --> 00:11:38.761
and I feel like so many
people get so wrapped up

00:11:38.761 --> 00:11:40.504
into what other people think

00:11:40.504 --> 00:11:42.966
that they ruin their entire lives

00:11:42.966 --> 00:11:46.799
living a life that they
don't even want to live.

00:11:50.369 --> 00:11:51.998
Everybody's got to find their own path

00:11:51.998 --> 00:11:54.833
and you have to realize that
when you get on that path,

00:11:54.833 --> 00:11:58.697
you're going to up, you're
going to make mistakes,

00:11:58.697 --> 00:12:02.114
you're going to have
bad ups like really bad

00:12:03.504 --> 00:12:05.508
but as long as you're willing to learn

00:12:05.508 --> 00:12:08.008
and you're willing to take
those as lessons about

00:12:08.008 --> 00:12:10.297
and you're smart enough to
not make those same mistakes

00:12:10.297 --> 00:12:12.714
over and over and over again,

00:12:13.815 --> 00:12:16.070
there's really nothing that you can't do.

00:12:16.070 --> 00:12:19.570
I see every day people with this mentality

00:12:20.862 --> 00:12:23.695
that the world owes them something

00:12:24.711 --> 00:12:27.988
and it's popular now to
talk about entitlement

00:12:27.988 --> 00:12:32.114
and everybody deserves and
all these different things

00:12:32.114 --> 00:12:35.397
that people like to bitch and cry about

00:12:35.397 --> 00:12:37.314
but people come at life

00:12:40.451 --> 00:12:42.868
with the wrong attitude, okay,

00:12:42.868 --> 00:12:45.113
and and they don't like hearing the truth

00:12:45.113 --> 00:12:47.454
